or case 2: with unresolved_without: [\"d0\"], the minimal_failure might be different because d0 cannot be tested alone? But d0 is not in required_failure_items (d4,d7). So maybe it doesn't matter. Or maybe unresolved_without indicates that d0 is required to be present for the failure to be resolved? Could be that the test is only valid if all items in unresolved_without are present? In case 2, unresolved_without has d0, so the test always includes d0? But then the minimal failure set would need to include d0 as well? That would be [\"d0\",\"d4\",\"d7\"]? But that's not minimal.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"unresolved_without\" means that if you remove that item from the set, the test becomes unresolved. That would imply that the test cannot determine pass/fail without that item. So to get a valid test, you must keep that item. So the minimal set must include all items in unresolved_without, plus the required_failure_items. In case 2, that would be [\"d0\",\"d4\",\"d7\"]? But then the minimal set might be that, but could there be a smaller subset that still fails? If we remove d0, the test becomes unresolved, so we cannot say it fails, so we must keep it. So minimal_failure would be [\"d0\",\"d4\",\"d7\"]. But then outcome=\"failure\", tests? Possibly the delta debugging algorithm would test various subsets and eventually conclude that.\n\nBut we need to verify with the first two cases where unresolved_without is empty, then minimal_failure would just be required_failure_items. That seems plausible.\n\nLet's assume that's the logic: The test function: For a given subset S of items, the test result is:\n- if S contains all items in unresolved_without (i.e., unresolved_without subset of S), then the test can be determined:\n   - if S contains all items in required_failure_items, then the test fails; otherwise passes.\n- else (if unresolved_without not subset of S), the test is unresolved.\n\nIn delta debugging, the algorithm typically handles unresolved by not splitting such sets. The goal is to find a minimal set that causes failure (i.e., test result fail), and that is minimal in the sense that removing any item from it either causes pass or unresolved. But if removing an item leads to unresolved, then we cannot consider that removal; the item is necessary for validity. So the minimal failure set must be a subset that includes all unresolved_without items and all required_failure_items? Not necessarily all required_failure_items are needed if there are overlaps.\n\nBut in case 2, required_failure_items = [d4,d7], unresolved_without=[d0]. So any valid test must include d0. The test fails if it includes d4 and d7. So the minimal failure set that is valid (includes d0) and fails (includes d4,d7) is any set that includes d0,d4,d7. But we need the minimal such set. Could we remove d0? No, because then test is unresolved, not fail. Could we remove d4? Then test passes. Could we remove d7? Then test passes. So the minimal set is {d0,d4,d7}. That seems plausible.\n\nNow tests count: We need to simulate the delta debugging algorithm procedure and count how many tests (subsets) are evaluated. The algorithm typically starts with the full set and recursively splits. We need to implement a specific algorithm? The problem statement says \"Return one JSON object ... The function must return a JSON-serializable object\". It doesn't specify the algorithm, but implies we should compute the output based on the input. There might be a standard algorithm that the evaluator expects. Perhaps the algorithm is the standard \"dd\" algorithm (Zeller's delta debugging). But we need to know the exact steps.\n\nGiven that it's a coding task, likely the implementation is straightforward and the outputs are deterministic based on the input parameters. Perhaps we can infer from the name \"SE-DD-01\" that it's about \"Delta Debugging 01\" and the algorithm is simple: find minimal set by iteratively removing items if possible, using a test function. But we need to implement the test function as described: using required_failure_items as the condition for failure, and unresolved_without as conditions for unresolved.\n\nThus, the solve function should:\n- Define a test function that takes a set of items and returns \"fail\", \"pass\", or \"unresolved\" based on the input case data.\n- Then run a delta debugging minimization algorithm (like the one described in \"Yesterday's Weather\" or the classic algorithm) to find a minimal failing set.\n- Count the number of tests performed (i.e., calls to test function) and return that as tests.\n- The outcome: if the final minimal set is non-empty and fails, then outcome = \"failure\". If it passes, outcome = \"pass\". But the algorithm assumes the full set fails (since required_failure_items are all present in the full set? Actually the full set includes all items, so it contains required_failure_items, and unresolved_without is subset of full set, so the full set fails. So outcome should be \"failure\".\n- minimal_failure is the list of items in the final minimal failing set.\n\nNow, we need to implement the delta debugging algorithm. There are variations. The classic algorithm (Zeller) works as follows:\n1. Start with the full set C (all items), and a granularity n=2.\n2. Repeat:\n   - Divide C into n subsets of roughly equal size.\n   - For each subset, test it.\n   - If any subset fails, then set C = that subset, n=2, continue.\n   - If any subset is unresolved, note but continue.\n   - If all subsets pass, then consider complements (i.e., test C without each subset).\n   - If any complement fails, set C = that complement, n=max(n-1,2), continue.\n   - If no complement fails, then increase n (n*=2) and repeat, unless n > |C|, then stop (C is minimal).\nThis is from Zeller's paper. But we need to handle unresolved. Typically, if a subset is unresolved, it is treated like pass? Actually, the algorithm avoids splitting unresolved subsets. So we need to adapt.\n\nGiven the limited info, I think the simplest expectation is that the test function is defined as:\n- If unresolved_without is a subset of S, then return \"fail\" if required_failure_items is subset of S, else \"pass\".\n- Otherwise return \"unresolved\".\n\nThen the delta debugging algorithm should find the minimal set. But we also need to count tests exactly as the algorithm proceeds.\n\nSince there is only one public fixture with 3 cases, we can try to implement a plausible algorithm and see if it produces consistent results across cases. However, we don't have expected outputs to verify. But the task is to write code that passes the evaluator's tests. We must trust that our interpretation is correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the task is simpler: just return minimal_failure as the set of required_failure_items plus any items from unresolved_without that are not already included? That would be trivial but then the tests count would be 0? But schema expects tests integer, so must be a calculated number.\n\nI lean towards the delta debugging simulation because it's a typical software engineering problem (delta debugging). The name \"SE-DD-01\" supports that.\n\nLet's outline implementation steps:\n\nDefine solve(case):\n- case contains items (list), required_failure_items (list), unresolved_without (list), _case_nonce (ignore).\n- Convert items to a set for convenience.\n- Define a function test(s): given a set of items (frozenset), compute:\n   - if not unresolved_without.issubset(s): return None (unresolved) or use a special value.\n   - else if required_failure_items.issubset(s): return \"fail\"\n   - else: return \"pass\"\n- Now implement delta debugging to find minimal failing set.\n- The algorithm should start with the full set (set(items)).\n- Initialize n=2, tests_count=0.\n- While True:\n   - Partition C into n subsets (if n > |C|, then break? Actually algorithm says if n > |C|, then minimal is found, but we also check complements).\n   - Test each subset. For each subset, increment