rministic results. We can simulate.\n\nBut we must be careful: The standard delta debugging algorithm may have variations. For instance, some implementations use a recursive splitting. We'll use the iterative version as described in Zeller's paper.\n\nLet's write pseudocode:\n\ndef minimize(C, test):\n    n = 2\n    tests = 0\n    while True:\n        # partition C into n subsets\n        parts = partition(C, n)\n        # test each part\n        for part in parts:\n            tests += 1\n            res = test(part)\n            if res == \"fail\":\n                C = part\n                n = 2\n                break\n        else: # no failing part\n            # test complements\n            for part in parts:\n                complement = C - part\n                tests += 1\n                res = test(complement)\n                if res == \"fail\":\n                    C = complement\n                    n = max(n-1, 2)\n                    break\n            else: # no failing complement\n                if n >= len(C):\n                    break\n                else:\n                    n = min(n*2, len(C))\n    return C, tests\n\nNote: We need to handle unresolved. In the above, if test returns None (unresolved), we should treat it like pass? According to Zeller, unresolved means we cannot conclude pass or fail, so we treat it as not failing (i.e., it does not cause a reduction). But in the algorithm, unresolved subsets are ignored for the purpose of failing, and unresolved complements are also ignored? Actually, the algorithm typically says if a complement is unresolved, it is treated as not failing. So we can treat any result other than \"fail\" as not failing. So we can adapt: test returns \"fail\", \"pass\", \"unresolved\". In the branching, only \"fail\" triggers a reduction. So we can treat \"unresolved\" just like pass for the control flow. So the above pseudocode works if we only check for \"fail\".\n\nBut we need to increment tests count for every test call, regardless of result.\n\nNow, we must implement partition: given a set/list C (ordered?), we need a deterministic order. Since items are strings like \"d0\",\"d1\",..., we can keep them sorted to have consistent order. The algorithm's output depends on order. Use sorted list of C.\n\npartition returns list of sets (or frozensets). For n subsets, we assign each element to one subset cyclically or by slicing. Classic algorithm often uses fair splitting: divide into n parts as evenly as possible. We'll use slicing: len(C) // n, remainder.\n\nNow, we need to handle case where unresolved_without is not a subset of some test. In test function, return None for unresolved. In delta debugging, if a subset is unresolved, it is not considered as failing, but does it affect the algorithm's progression? The algorithm typically continues. For complements, if the complement is unresolved, it's also not failing. So fine.\n\nNow, we need to test this on the given fixtures. Let's manually simulate for case 0:\nitems = [d0..d7], required=[d1,d4], unresolved=[]\nC = {d0,d1,d2,d3,d4,d5,d6,d7}\nn=2, partition into two subsets of size 4 each. Sorted: [d0,d1,d2,d3] and [d4,d5,d6,d7] (assuming order). Test subset1: contains d1 but not d4, so pass? Actually required is both d1 and d4, so subset1 misses d4 => pass. Subset2: contains d4 but not d1 => pass. No fail. Then test complements: complement1 = C - subset1 = {d4,d5,d6,d7} => contains d4 but not d1 => pass. complement2 = C - subset2 = {d0,d1,d2,d3} => contains d1 but not d4 => pass. No fail. n < len(C)? n=2 < 8, so set n=4. Next iteration: partition C into 4 subsets of size 2 each: [d0,d1], [d2,d3], [d4,d5], [d6,d7]. Test each: [d0,d1] contains d1 but not d4 => pass; [d2,d3] contains neither => pass; [d4,d5] contains d4 but not d1 => pass; [d6,d7] none => pass. Then complements: complement of [d0,d1] = {d2,d3,d4,d5,d6,d7} contains d1? No (since removed d1), contains d4 => but d1 missing, so pass. complement of [d2,d3] = {d0,d1,d4,d5,d6,d7} contains both d1 and d4? Yes, contains d1 and d4, so should fail? Wait: complement of [d2,d3] is C minus {d2,d3} = {d0,d1,d4,d5,d6,d7}. That set includes d1 and d4, so test fails. So we have a failing complement. Then set C = that complement (size 6), n = max(2-1,2)=2? Actually n was 4, but we found complement failing, so we set n = max(n-1,2) = max(3,2)=3. Then continue with new C = {d0,d1,d4,d5,d6,d7} and n=3. Continue algorithm. Eventually we might reduce to {d1,d4}. We'll need to simulate further. This seems plausible and yields a number of tests.\n\nGiven the complexity, I think implementing the classic delta debugging algorithm is the intended solution.\n\nBut we must ensure we correctly handle unresolved_without. For case 2, unresolved_without = [\"d0\"]. So any test not containing d0 will return unresolved. In the algorithm, when testing subsets of C that may not contain d0, they will be unresolved, not fail, so they won't be chosen as reducing sets. Only subsets that contain d0 and also contain d4 and d7 will fail. That will likely lead to minimal set {d0,d4,d7}. The number of tests will be different.\n\nThus, the implementation seems consistent.\n\nNow, we need to write the Python code. It will define solve(case) that returns dict. We'll use only standard library. No random, no external.\n\nImplementation details:\n- case is dict; items, required_failure_items, unresolved_without are lists.\n- Convert to sets for efficient subset checks.\n- Define test function that accepts a frozenset and returns \"fail\", \"pass\", or \"unresolved\". Use predefined required_set and unresolved_set from case.\n- Use a list representation for C to allow ordering. We'll keep C as sorted list of strings (or set for operations). We'll need to convert to frozenset for testing.\n- Implement partition as described.\n- Use recursion? Iterative loop as above.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The algorithm may loop indefinitely if not careful with n increment and break cond